By embedding the outbox in the same transaction as the domain update, the pattern eliminates the need for a distributed transaction coordinator while still providing strong consistency guarantees. The subsequent sections will contrast this approach with Saga orchestration, exposing the nuanced trade‑offs that inform the final architectural decision.

## 🧩 Saga Pattern Deep Dive

A saga is a long‑running transaction composed of a sequence of local operations, each of which can be compensated if a later step fails. Unlike the outbox, which relies on a single atomic write, a saga distributes work across multiple services while preserving eventual consistency. The pattern is most effective when the domain model spans independent bounded contexts that cannot be locked in a single database transaction.

### 1. Domain Model

Below is a minimal Java OOP representation of a saga orchestrator. The model separates the *definition* of a step from its *execution* logic, allowing each microservice to expose a lightweight interface.

```java
public interface SagaStep {
    void execute() throws SagaException;
    void compensate() throws SagaException;
    StepStatus status();
}

public enum StepStatus { PENDING, COMPLETED, FAILED, COMPENSATED }

public abstract class AbstractSagaStep implements SagaStep {
    protected StepStatus status = StepStatus.PENDING;
    protected final String stepId;

    protected AbstractSagaStep(String stepId) { this.stepId = stepId; }

    @Override public StepStatus status() { return status; }
}

public class DebitAccountStep extends AbstractSagaStep {
    private final AccountService accountService;
    private final long amount;

    public DebitAccountStep(String id, AccountService svc, long amt) {
        super(id); this.accountService = svc; this.amount = amt;
    }

    @Override public void execute() throws SagaException {
        accountService.debit(amount);
        status = StepStatus.COMPLETED;
    }

    @Override public void compensate() throws SagaException {
        accountService.credit(amount);
        status = StepStatus.COMPENSATED;
    }
}
```

Other steps (`ReserveInventoryStep`, `ChargePaymentStep`, etc.) follow the same pattern. The orchestrator maintains an ordered list of steps and a state machine that drives execution and compensation.

### 2. Orchestrator Service

```java
@Service
public class OrderSagaOrchestrator {
    private final List<SagaStep> steps = new ArrayList<>();

    public void startSaga(OrderRequest req) {
        steps.add(new DebitAccountStep("step1", accountSvc, req.getAmount()));
        steps.add(new ReserveInventoryStep("step2", inventorySvc, req.getItems()));
        steps.add(new ChargePaymentStep("step3", paymentSvc, req.getPaymentInfo()));

        runSaga();
    }

    private void runSaga() {
        for (int i = 0; i < steps.size(); i++) {
            try {
                steps.get(i).execute();
            } catch (SagaException e) {
                compensate(i);
                throw new RuntimeException("Saga aborted", e);
            }
        }
    }

    private void compensate(int failedIndex) {
        for (int j = failedIndex - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
            try { steps.get(j).compensate(); } catch (SagaException ignored) {}
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. REST Endpoint

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
public class OrderController {
    private final OrderSagaOrchestrator orchestrator;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Void> placeOrder(@RequestBody OrderRequest req) {
        orchestrator.startSaga(req);
        return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
    }
}
```

The controller simply forwards the request to the orchestrator; the heavy lifting is done asynchronously.

